WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.823 --> 00:00:18.224
still appeared quite shaken as we sat in my study.

2
00:00:18.985 --> 00:00:22.467
“Professor Moriarty has such a quiet manner, no one would ever suspect he’s a villain.”

3
00:00:22.487 --> 00:00:25.809
“If you haven’t contacted the police, you should,” I said.

4
00:00:25.829 --> 00:00:27.530
“That would be useless.

5
00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.952
I already have proof that my death will look like an accident.”

6
00:00:31.808 --> 00:00:33.389
“What has that evil genius done to you?”

7
00:00:33.949 --> 00:00:34.849
I cried in dismay.

8
00:00:34.869 --> 00:00:38.450
“Professor Moriarty didn’t waste any time,” Holmes said.

9
00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.871
“Around noon today I went out to do an errand.

10
00:00:41.731 --> 00:00:45.952
As I crossed Oxford Street, a two-horse van rounded the corner and headed straight for me.

11
00:00:46.912 --> 00:00:50.333
I sprang for the sidewalk and saved myself by a fraction of a second.”

12
00:00:51.354 --> 00:00:53.334
“Did you provide the police with a description of the driver?”

13
00:00:53.754 --> 00:00:54.194
I asked.

14
00:00:56.255 --> 00:00:59.116
“No, the van disappeared in an instant,” Holmes said.

15
00:01:00.308 --> 00:01:02.789
“I kept to the sidewalk after that, but I still wasn’t safe.

16
00:01:03.889 --> 00:01:07.410
As I walked down another street, a brick fell from the roof of a house and shattered at

17
00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:07.870
my feet.

18
00:01:09.131 --> 00:01:10.671
At that point I did call the police.”

19
00:01:10.691 --> 00:01:12.631
“Did they catch anyone?”

20
00:01:13.712 --> 00:01:14.512
Holmes shook his head.

21
00:01:14.532 --> 00:01:19.413
“They searched the house and found piles of brick and slate on the roof, ready for a repair

22
00:01:19.453 --> 00:01:24.355
job.” The police tried to persuade Holmes that the wind had toppled the brick from the roof.

23
00:01:25.455 --> 00:01:28.476
Although Holmes doubted that explanation, he couldn’t prove anything.

24
00:01:29.568 --> 00:01:33.450
He spent the rest of the day at the rooms of his brother, Mycroft, but that wasn’t the

25
00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:34.150
end of his troubles.

26
00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:38.832
“On my way here, I was attacked by a guy with a club.”

27
00:01:39.692 --> 00:01:41.553
Holmes continued calmly telling his story.

28
00:01:41.573 --> 00:01:44.814
“I knocked him down, scraping my knuckles on his front teeth.

29
00:01:46.235 --> 00:01:48.936
The police arrested him, but I’m sure he’ll soon be released.”

30
00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:53.858
“Surely the police know that Moriarty has a gang of thugs and that he’s behind these attacks?”

31
00:01:53.878 --> 00:01:58.420
“There’s no evidence to connect my attacker with the mild-mannered professor.

32
00:01:59.764 --> 00:02:04.567
At the moment only Inspector Lestrade of Scotland Yard is fully aware of Moriarty’s criminal network.

33
00:02:05.648 --> 00:02:08.690
And we don’t want to do anything to alarm Moriarty before Monday.”

34
00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:12.213
“You must spend the night here with me,” I insisted.

35
00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:15.074
“No, my friend, I’m a dangerous guest.

36
00:02:16.175 --> 00:02:19.057
I don’t want my presence to lure Moriarty’s thugs to your door.”

37
00:02:19.978 --> 00:02:20.778
Holmes shook his head.

38
00:02:20.798 --> 00:02:25.742
“The best thing would be for me to escape from London for a few days.

39
00:02:26.882 --> 00:02:30.485
Once the police have arrested Moriarty and his gang, I’ll be able to safely return.

40
00:02:31.646 --> 00:02:35.249
In the meantime it would be a great help—and pleasure—if you would accompany me.”

41
00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:38.952
I was flattered that Holmes had turned to me in this crisis.

42
00:02:38.973 --> 00:02:43.376
“I don’t have a lot of patients right now, and my wife is away, so I’d be glad

43
00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:43.957
to come along.”

44
00:02:45.018 --> 00:02:46.059
“Can you leave tomorrow morning?”

45
00:02:47.059 --> 00:02:48.641
“Well if necessary.”

46
00:02:48.661 --> 00:02:50.943
“Oh, it’s absolutely necessary.

47
00:02:51.904 --> 00:02:56.287
Never forget that you are facing the cleverest rogue and the most powerful group of criminals I’ve ever

48
00:02:56.347 --> 00:02:59.253
encountered.” Holmes glanced at the window.

49
00:02:59.273 --> 00:03:02.074
“That is why I immediately closed your shutters.

50
00:03:02.094 --> 00:03:06.936
“Now listen carefully because you must do exactly what I tell you.”

51
00:03:08.156 --> 00:03:12.098
After Holmes gave me his detailed instructions, I tried again to get him to stay overnight.

52
00:03:13.059 --> 00:03:14.879
But he insisted on leaving immediately.

53
00:03:14.899 --> 00:03:20.602
“If you’ll just show me to your back door,” he said, “I’ll climb over the rear wall of

54
00:03:20.642 --> 00:03:22.463
your garden and leave you out of harm’s way.”

55
00:03:23.723 --> 00:03:27.798
I led Holmes outside, where he scaled the wall that led to the street behind my house.

56
00:03:29.079 --> 00:03:31.301
He whistled for a cab and vanished into the night.

57
00:03:32.703 --> 00:03:34.965
I went back into the house and packed my suitcase.

58
00:03:36.246 --> 00:03:39.128
Then I had a trusted messenger take my luggage to Victoria Station.

59
00:03:40.289 --> 00:03:44.773
After breakfast the next morning, I too left my house by cab, although I used the front door.

60
00:03:46.755 --> 00:03:52.400
Following Holmes’ orders, I was careful not to take the first cab—or even the second—that responded to my

61
00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:56.490
whistle. I didn’t want to jump into a cab that had been lying in wait for me.

62
00:03:57.772 --> 00:04:01.775
I ordered the cab to stop at the edge of the crowded market that Holmes had specified and

63
00:04:01.835 --> 00:04:02.896
handed the driver the fare.

64
00:04:04.017 --> 00:04:08.741
Then I ran at top speed through the market, arriving at exactly quarter past nine, as Holmes had

65
00:04:08.801 --> 00:04:13.485
insisted. At the curb I found the carriage that Holmes had said would be waiting for me.

66
00:04:14.907 --> 00:04:18.890
Although the driver turned his face away from me, Holmes had said he would be wearing a black

67
00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:20.712
cloak with a red-trimmed collar.

68
00:04:21.868 --> 00:04:24.109
We immediately sped off for Victoria Station.

69
00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:27.891
So far everything had gone according to plan.

70
00:04:29.031 --> 00:04:31.512
My suitcase was waiting for me at the left-luggage office.

71
00:04:32.573 --> 00:04:37.295
I had no trouble finding the Paris express train or the first-class compartment that Holmes had reserved for

72
00:04:37.395 --> 00:04:40.896
us. My only source of anxiety was Holmes himself.

73
00:04:41.857 --> 00:04:44.878
The train was due to leave in seven minutes, and there was no sign of him.

74
00:04:45.779 --> 00:04:48.540
I got off the train and searched among the travelers on the platform.

75
00:04:49.428 --> 00:04:52.230
As I scanned the passing faces, I felt a tug on my sleeve.

76
00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:56.112
I turned to find an elderly priest looking up at me hopefully.

77
00:04:56.132 --> 00:04:57.833
“Help, please?”

78
00:04:58.253 --> 00:05:01.495
The priest spoke with an Italian accent and gestured toward a nearby porter.

79
00:05:01.515 --> 00:05:05.437
“I can’t understand what he wants,” the porter said to me.

80
00:05:07.319 --> 00:05:10.641
The priest said something in Italian while pointing to his suitcase and the train.

81
00:05:11.821 --> 00:05:13.622
The only word I understood was “Paris.”

82
00:05:13.642 --> 00:05:16.284
“I’m not certain,” I said.

83
00:05:17.544 --> 00:05:20.946
“But I think he wants to be sure that he and his luggage are on the express train

84
00:05:20.986 --> 00:05:21.486
to Paris.”

85
00:05:22.647 --> 00:05:26.289
The priest nodded enthusiastically, and I left him to work things out with the porter.

86
00:05:27.449 --> 00:05:30.531
After a quick look around the station, I reluctantly boarded the train.

87
00:05:31.572 --> 00:05:34.473
I was terribly afraid that something bad had happened to Holmes overnight.

88
00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:39.396
Imagine my annoyance when I found the priest in the compartment that Holmes had reserved for us.

89
00:05:40.837 --> 00:05:44.118
I tried to explain that he was in the wrong place, but he couldn’t understand me.

90
00:05:45.059 --> 00:05:47.060
The doors to the train were shut, and the whistle blew.

91
00:05:49.492 --> 00:05:53.755
As I continued to look out the window for Holmes, the priest spoke to me in perfect English.

92
00:05:53.775 --> 00:05:57.398
“My dear Watson, you haven’t even said good morning to me.”

93
00:05:58.398 --> 00:06:02.141
The drooping priest straightened up, his dull eyes suddenly full of fire.

94
00:06:03.262 --> 00:06:04.763
“Holmes!” I cried.

95
00:06:04.783 --> 00:06:06.444
“You startled me!”

96
00:06:06.464 --> 00:06:08.445
“We must still be careful.”

97
00:06:09.366 --> 00:06:12.068
Holmes once again shrank into the character of the old priest.

98
00:06:12.088 --> 00:06:16.131
“I took the trouble of disguising myself because they’re hot on our trail.”

99
00:06:16.151 --> 00:06:17.912
“What do you mean?”

100
00:06:18.576 --> 00:06:19.417
I asked with alarm.

101
00:06:20.357 --> 00:06:24.139
Holmes peered at the platform and drew my attention to a very tall, thin man.

102
00:06:24.159 --> 00:06:27.500
“Ah, there is Moriarty himself!”
